w\n1 · The offline re-verification algorithm has a race that will fail legitimate sessions. §15 step 4 asserts "First event in segment has prev_hash == chain_head_at_arrival." That only holds if no other session's mutation lands between the head read at step 3 of the mint and the arrival event being appended. On a chain at ~1.9M events with concurrent sessions, it frequently won't. The genesis commits to a head that existed, not to the immediate predecessor. Verification should assert the committed head is an ancestor of the segment's first event, not its parent.\n\n2 · Two genesis-derivation sites are unaccounted for, and the rationale for keeping etch_footprint is wrong. §6.2 says etch_footprint survives, renamed etch_chain_footprint, because "its only consumath." It isn't.Its live callers are identity_sled_dispatch.rs:388 and :428 —two places that atch time — plusanna_scribe and write_sled_advance, both being deleted. The  snowball path ever touches it.So: 388 and 428 are two more authors of the identity anchor  that the design'ile doesn'tmention (it only covers get_identity, write_identity and the :465 writer), anotprint has noconsumers at all — renaming it to something                snowball-sounded dead function.\n                                                           3 · The authorits, and the readpath contradicts the diagram. §5 names the authority as    "sealed per-sess/dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/identity_sled.<hash16>.blob — the catalog is oession; thatpath is the identity_sled plugin's blob holding all sleds. Then the per-reqhe in-processstate cache, which the diagram labels a projection. So the thing declared abeing read.\n\nTwo consequencesssion gets itsown blob (which changes what "a plugin exists ⟺ its blob is in the catalog" meawhenmutation_index advances — if the whole identity_sled blob  re-seals per mutery mutation onthe box, under concurrent readers.                         \n4 · The genesis is stored twice in one record. §12 has bothgenesi